Embedded React: let the host draw opaque images as layers beneath the framebuffer.

Modified from Embedded React (Apache-2.0, Copyright 2026 Cory Lamming) at the
revision pinned in dependencies.lock.json, after the patches listed before this
one there. Changed files: backends/software/renderer_backend.c,
bridges/quickjs/js/src/embedded-react/index.d.ts,
bridges/quickjs/js/src/props.js, bridges/quickjs/native_ui_bridge.c,
engine/animation/animation.c, engine/core/native_renderer.c,
engine/core/renderer_internal.h, engine/include/er_scene.h,
engine/include/native_renderer.h, engine/scene/compositor.c,
engine/scene/er_node_internal.h and engine/tests/rendering/CMakeLists.txt; new
file engine/tests/rendering/test_layers.c.
Not proposed upstream: PS5 React maintains this change here.

A crossfade between two full-screen images rasterized both images and every
translucent node over them on each frame: at 3840x2160 about 30 ms of raster
per frame on eight PS5 cores. An Image with the new `layer` prop can instead
be drawn by the host. When the host enables it (er_set_layers_enabled) and the
image qualifies, the compositor clears the image's rect to transparent black
(the new optional backend clear_rect) and lists the image, its crop, rect,
clip and opacity in er_get_layers(); the host draws the layers in paint order,
then the framebuffer over them with premultiplied source-over. Opacity changes
on the containers whose only content is a layer repaint nothing.

An image qualifies when it is opaque, untinted, square-cornered, drawn with
cover or stretch, has no transform beyond translation on it or an ancestor,
and every ancestor below full opacity holds nothing else. Below full opacity
it must also sit directly on a qualifying layer (its container the preceding
sibling of that one's) that, through the layers under it, starts with an
opaque layer covering it, so nothing rasterized shows through it. Otherwise
it is rasterized as before. A node that changes between the two repaints its
box. Without a host that enables layers nothing changes.

The software backend's blends now carry the destination alpha (source-over
on all four premultiplied channels) instead of forcing it to 0xFF, so what is
drawn over a cleared rect keeps its coverage. Over an opaque destination the
result is bit for bit what it was.

test_layers crossfades two layer images under a translucent overlay on the
software backend and checks that the framebuffer drawn over the layers looks
like the same scene rasterized with layers off at each step, that the fade
paints nothing, and that a translucent layer without an opaque layer directly
under it, or with other content between, is rasterized.

